7. BeanFactory vs ApplicationContext
---------------------------------------

76. What is BeanFactory?
-->BeanFactory is the basic/root IoC container in Spring.

Its main responsibility is to:

              Create beans
              Configure beans
              Manage bean dependencies
              Provide beans when requested

For example:
------------ 
        BeanFactory factory = new ClassPathXmlApplicationContext("beans.xml");
        
        Student student = factory.getBean(Student.class);

Here:
      BeanFactory
           ↓
      Spring IoC Container
           ↓
      Creates and manages beans


Interview answer
----------------
BeanFactory is the basic IoC container provided by Spring. It is responsible for creating, configuring, and managing beans and their
dependencies.


Important point
----------------
BeanFactory is the basic container, while ApplicationContext provides additional features on top of it.











77. What is ApplicationContext?
-->ApplicationContext is an advanced Spring IoC container.

It provides all the basic bean-management capabilities of BeanFactory, plus additional features such as:

            Event handling
            Internationalization (i18n)
            Resource loading
            Application-level services
            Better integration with Spring's ecosystem
            Automatic processing of many framework features
            
            For example, in Spring Boot, you normally interact with an ApplicationContext indirectly.
            
            ApplicationContext context =
                    new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(AppConfig.class);
            
            Student student = context.getBean(Student.class);



Interview answer
-----------------
ApplicationContext is an advanced IoC container in Spring that extends the capabilities of BeanFactory and provides additional
features such as event handling, internationalization, resource loading, and integration with Spring's other features.











78. Difference between BeanFactory and ApplicationContext?
-->
| BeanFactory                                        | ApplicationContext                                                 |
| -------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Basic IoC container                                | Advanced IoC container                                             |
| Provides basic bean management                     | Provides bean management + additional features                     |
| Generally lazy initialization by default           | Singleton beans are generally eagerly initialized by default       |
| Supports basic dependency injection                | Supports dependency injection plus many additional Spring features |
| Less commonly used directly in modern applications | Commonly used in Spring/Spring Boot applications                   |
| Lightweight/basic container                        | More feature-rich container                                        |


Think of it like this:
----------------------
          BeanFactory
               ↓
          Basic Spring container
               ↓
          Create + configure + manage beans

Where as:
---------
        ApplicationContext
             ↓
        BeanFactory capabilities
             +
        Events
        +
        i18n
        +
        Resource loading
        +
        Application features
        +
        More Spring integration



Interview answer
-----------------
BeanFactory provides the basic IoC container functionality, while ApplicationContext is a more feature-rich container that extends
BeanFactory and provides features such as event handling, internationalization, resource loading, and better integration with Spring.









79. What is lazy loading?
-->Lazy loading means:
            An object/bean is created only when it is actually needed or requested.

For example:
          @Component
          @Lazy
          public class PaymentService {
          
              public PaymentService() {
                  System.out.println("PaymentService created");
              }
          }



If the application starts:
--------------------------
        Application starts
               ↓
        PaymentService is NOT created yet
        
        When you actually request/use the bean:
        
        getBean(PaymentService.class)
               ↓
        PaymentService object is created

So:
----

      Start application
            ↓
      Don't create yet
            ↓
      Actually request bean
            ↓
      Create bean
      Simple real-world example
      
      Imagine a restaurant that prepares a dish only after you order it.
      
      Customer orders
            ↓
      Dish is prepared

That's similar to lazy loading.



Interview answer
-----------------
Lazy loading means the bean is created only when it is actually requested or needed, rather than during application startup.












80. What is eager loading?
-->Eager loading is the opposite.

It means:
----------
The bean is created during application/container startup, even if it has not yet been requested.

For example, singleton beans in an ApplicationContext are generally initialized eagerly by default.

Conceptually:
---------------
Application starts
       ↓
Spring creates bean
       ↓
Application ready




You don't have to call:
-----------------------            
            getBean()
            
            for the bean to be initialized.
            
            Real-world example
            
            Restaurant prepares commonly ordered dishes before customers order them.
            
            Restaurant opens
                  ↓
            Dish prepared
                  ↓
            Customer orders
                  ↓
            Already available



Interview answer
-----------------
Eager loading means the bean is created during application startup, before it is explicitly requested. Singleton beans in 
ApplicationContext are eagerly initialized by default unless configured otherwise.













81. Why ApplicationContext is preferred?
-->BeanFactory provides basic functionality, but modern Spring applications generally use ApplicationContext because it provides many
    additional features.

For example:

1. Event handling

Spring supports application events:

Application
    ↓
Event
    ↓
Listener
2. Internationalization

ApplicationContext supports messages for different languages.

For example:

English → "Welcome"
French  → "Bienvenue"
3. Resource loading

It can work with resources such as:

XML files
Properties files
Files
Classpath resources
4. Better Spring integration

It integrates naturally with many Spring features such as:

@Component
@Service
@Repository
@Autowired
@Configuration
@Bean

and many other framework facilities.

5. Eager initialization

Singleton beans are generally initialized during startup, allowing configuration/dependency problems to be detected earlier.

Interview answer

ApplicationContext is preferred because it provides all the basic bean-management capabilities of BeanFactory along with additional 
features such as event handling, internationalization, resource loading, and better integration with the Spring ecosystem.









82. Types of ApplicationContext?
-->There are several implementations of ApplicationContext.

The important ones to know for interviews are:

1. ClassPathXmlApplicationContext

Used when configuration is provided through an XML file located in the classpath.

ApplicationContext context =
    new ClassPathXmlApplicationContext("beans.xml");

Example:

src/main/resources/
        |
        └── beans.xml
2. FileSystemXmlApplicationContext

Used when the XML configuration file is located using a filesystem path.

ApplicationContext context =
    new FileSystemXmlApplicationContext("C:/config/beans.xml");
3. AnnotationConfigApplicationContext

Used for Java-based configuration.

For example:

@Configuration
@ComponentScan("com.example")
public class AppConfig {
}

Then:

ApplicationContext context =
    new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(AppConfig.class);

This is particularly relevant to modern Spring applications.

4. WebApplicationContext

Used in web applications.

It provides ApplicationContext functionality specifically for web environments and integrates with the web application lifecycle.

In modern Spring MVC applications, you may encounter implementations such as:

AnnotationConfigWebApplicationContext
